1550| 	 *  the only limit on a guest LBA was a byte-count cap, so ONE
1551| 	 *  WRITE(10) past capacity grew a 10,240-byte image to 512,000,512
1552| 	 *  bytes -- and the growth persisted, because capacity is re-derived
1553| 	 *  by stat() on the next run.
1554| 	 *
1555| 	 *  THE TWO LIMITS DIFFER ON PURPOSE, and the asymmetry is the whole
1556| 	 *  design:
1557| 	 *
1558| 	 *    WRITES are bounded by d->total_size, the BACKED extent, so a
1559| 	 *    guest can never grow the host file by even one byte.
1560| 	 *
1561| 	 *    READS are bounded by the ADVERTISED capacity, which is rounded up
1562| 	 *    to whole cylinders and so exceeds the file by 480..992 blocks on
1563| 	 *    every image in this project.  Reads of that gap must keep
1564| 	 *    succeeding as zero-fill: the pmax rig's own BSD disklabel records
1565| 	 *    d_secperunit = 614880 against a 614400-block file, with partition
1566| 	 *    c extending 480 blocks past the end -- geometry the guest's own
1567| 	 *    installer wrote.  Bounding reads by total_size instead was
1568| 	 *    measured to break all five rig images.
1569| 	 *
1570| 	 *  Refusing gap WRITES costs nothing measurable: since the shipped
1571| 	 *  code had no bound at all, any write there would have grown the file
1572| 	 *  permanently at some point in each image's history, and all five
1573| 	 *  still sit at their exact original sizes (300 MiB, 300 MiB, 1 GiB,
1574| 	 *  1 GiB, 2 GiB).  Recorded honestly: this does leave READ CAPACITY
1575| 	 *  advertising blocks the disk will then refuse to write, which
1576| 	 *  disklabel(8) or fsck on a raw partition could reach.  It cannot be
1577| 	 *  fixed by advertising less, because the advertised number is already
1578| 	 *  baked into guest-written labels on disk.
1579| 	 *
1580| 	 *  Tapes are exempt: their offsets are sequential positions, not
1581| 	 *  addresses into a fixed extent (the SCSI tape read uses
1582| 	 *  d->tape_offset and is legitimately unbounded).
1583| 	 *
1584| 	 *  The comparison is written to avoid offset+len, which would overflow
1585| 	 *  before it could be tested.  The zero-fill on the read side is
1586| 	 *  LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness: dev_wdc's read path declares a 32 KB
1587| 	 *  buffer on the stack, discards this function's return value, and
1588| 	 *  copies the whole buffer to the guest, so an early return that left
1589| 	 *  buf untouched would hand the guest uninitialised host memory.
1590| 	 */
1591| 	if (!d->is_a_tape) {
1592| 		int64_t limit = writeflag ? d->total_size
1593| 		    : (int64_t) d->nr_of_logical_blocks * d->logical_block_size;
1594| 
1595| 		if (offset < 0 || (int64_t)len > limit ||
1596| 		    (int64_t)offset > limit - (int64_t)len) {
1597| 			if (!writeflag)
1598| 				memset(buf, 0, len);
